# What never reaches the build context.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the one that bites. The obvious one is size:
# a venv or an htmlcov directory in the context is megabytes uploaded to the
# daemon on every build for nothing. The other is cache invalidation: the
# Dockerfile's `COPY app/` is the layer that changes on every commit, and
# anything swept into the context that changes more often than the source does
# would invalidate layers that had no reason to rebuild.
#
# Nothing here is a substitute for the Dockerfile copying only what it needs.
# The build copies `requirements.txt` and `app/` and nothing else, so tests and
# dev dependencies stay out of the image whatever this file says. This is the
# belt to that file's braces.

# Version control and tooling
.git
.gitignore
.github
**/.DS_Store

# Python build and cache artifacts
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/

# Virtual environments. Named broadly because the README has used both
# `venv/` and `.venv/` at different times.
venv/
.venv/
env/
.env
.env.*

# Test and coverage output
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
tests/

# Development-only dependency pins. Never installed by the Dockerfile; excluded
# so a stray `COPY . .` in a future edit cannot pick them up either.
requirements-dev.txt

# Local development scaffolding, none of which belongs in a Lambda image.
compose.yaml
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
scripts/
README.md
pytest.ini

# Editor state
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
